# Vault .gitignore.
#
# Every vault needs one, for a reason that is not obvious: a machine-wide gitignore that
# hides CLAUDE.md and .claude/ (a common setup for people who keep agent config out of
# client repos) will hide them here too, and a vault whose conventions file is untracked
# loses the one thing its history is for. The negations below override that.

# Override a global gitignore that hides agent config - this IS the vault's content
!CLAUDE.md
!.claude/
!.claude/**
!.mcp.json

# ...but never Claude Code runtime state
.claude/*.lock

# Python bytecode (scripts in resources/scripts/ run in place)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Defensive: never commit a secret if one is dropped into the vault by mistake.
# Real secrets live in ~/.paraos/secrets/, outside any synced folder - this is the last
# line of defence for that rule, because a credential written into a vault leaks the
# moment the vault syncs to a cloud drive or a git remote.
**/config.json
**/cookies.json
**/cookies.txt
**/.env

# The broad cookie glob is scoped to where only code lives. Vault-wide it would also hide
# real content - an event photo named "cookies.jpg" - which is how a guard like this
# quietly drops files out of a repo. Narrow globs above are not a substitute: they miss
# session-cookie.txt, which is exactly the shape a scraper writes.
resources/scripts/**/*cookie*
